#inference <inference-rules>

@inference-rules gives the syntax-directed version of the declarative rules from @declarative-rules. T-★-intro has no counterpart, since the algorithm never discards a type and T-eq has none either, since row unification already works up to ≈. T-let is discussed in @let-generalization. A-cons and A-λ-I are standard. A-rec and the A-ξ rules thread the state through a record body and build its row. A-λ-E and A-conc draw fresh variables for the shape their premises demand and give the resulting equations to $#st_solve$. The judgement $S ⊢ τ ≐ τ' ⇝ S'$ runs the unifier of @unification and writes its solution into the state and $S ⊢ τ ≐ τ′ #st_solve S′$ does the same and _saturates_ afterwards.

A-var instantiates a qualified scheme with fresh variables at the sorts of its binders and replays the instantiated stumps by $scripts(↝)^(*!)$. A scheme stump carries no blocker, so the replay $scripts(↝)^*$ computes one: it answers the stumps that the instance already decides and parks the others under the variables that block them. The definition fo this judgement is deffered to @app-replay. Saturation $#st_sat$ follows the replay, since a replayed stump whose result is already solved can write a row solution and make a stump stale that the replay has already parked.

As in @declarative-rules, three selection rules cover the three verdicts of the lookup judgement. Each rule infers a type for $e$, unifies it with ${β}$ for a fresh row-variable β and runs the lookup on the solved row $⟦S₂⟧β$. A-sel and A-sel-⊥ use the verdict directly but A-sel-\? is where the stump mechanism enters. Its premise $(⟦S₂⟧β).l ↓ #h(0.2em) ? "on" α$ states that the lookup answers $?$ and names its blocker $α$. The rule does not commit ★, but returns a fresh type-variable δ as a placeholder for the result and parks the stump under $α$.

== Let-Generalization <let-generalization>

#let generalization = figure(
  caption: "Generalization.",
  stack(
    spacing: 14pt,
    flexbox(
      derive(
        "A-let",
        stack(
          spacing: 8pt,
          $Γ; S₁ ⊢ e₁ ⇒ τ₁; S₂ #h(2em) macron(α) = "gen"_(Γ, S₁)(S₂, τ₁)$,
          $Γ · (x: ∀(macron(α): S₂(macron(α))). ⟦S₂⟧Δ_q ⇒ ⟦S₂⟧τ₁); S₂ #st_drop Δ_q ⊢ e₂ ⇒ τ₂; S₃$,
        ),
        $Γ; S₁ ⊢ #b[let] x = e₁ #b[in] e₂ ⇒ τ₂; S₃$,
      ),
    ),
    align(
      left,
      $
        Δ_q &= {p ∈ Δ₂ | "blocker"(p) ∈ macron(α)} #h(3em) Δ_Γ = Δ₂ #st_drop Δ_q \
        "gen"_(Γ, S₁)(S₂, τ₁) &= "the greatest" macron(α) ⊆ "ftv"(⟦S₂⟧τ₁, ⟦S₂⟧Δ₂) "such that" \
        & #h(1em) macron(α) ∩ "ftv"(⟦S₂⟧(Γ, Δ_Γ)) = ∅ \
        & #h(1em) "ftv"(⟦S₂⟧"results"(Δ_q)) ⊆ macron(α) \
        & #h(1em) Δ_q ∩ Δ₁ = ∅ \
      $,
    ),
  ),
)
#generalization <generalization>

@generalization gives A-let. The rule infers $e₁$ binds the scheme and continues with $e₂$. The choice of the generalized variables $macron(α)$ is delegated to the function $"gen"_(Γ, S₁)$ that partitions the stumps into two groups. $Δ_q$ is the list of stumps blocked on a variable of the scheme, and these stumps go into the scheme as its qualifiers Q. $Δ_Γ = Δ₂ #st_drop Δ_q$ are the remaining stumps, which stay in the solver state. The partition relies on quiescence because otherwise, a stump might be blocked on a solved variable and not on one of $macron(α)$. It would then stay in $Δ_Γ$ and the lookup variable would not be generalized.

The tree lines in $"gen"_(Γ, S₁)$ make a choice of $macron(α)$ admissible. The first codition ensures that all variables are fresh for the environment which consists of Γ together with the stumps that stay parked. It is the same condition as in Hindley-Milner generalization @damas_milner. The second condition makes sure that all stump results (δ) are itself generalized as they are part of the stumps. The last condition ensures that $Δ_q$ only contains stumps parked while inferring $e₁$ because a stump that is captured from the outside would otherwise never be instantiated.

The algorithm computes the set of variables by deleting those that don't hold the statements so a failing condition never makes the binding monomorphic completely monomorphic but only parts of it.

#example(name: [Generalizing a selection])[
  Consider `let f = x: x.l in f {l = c}`. For the bound term, A-λ-I gives x a fresh γ, and A-sel-\? unifies γ with ${β}$ for a fresh β, finds that $β.l$ answers $?$ on β, and parks $⟨β ⊳ β.l ↓ δ⟩$ with a fresh δ. The bound term has type ${β} → δ$. Since γ is solved, $macron(α) = β, δ$. The single stump moves into the scheme, and nothing stays parked. f is bound to $∀(β: "Row", δ: "Type"). ⟨β.l ↓ δ⟩ ⇒ {β} → δ$, which is selQ of @principality. At the use, A-var instantiates the scheme with fresh β′ and δ′ and replays the stump, which is still blocked and is parked on β′. A-λ-E then solves $β′ ≔ (l: 𝓫_c)$ and identifies δ′ with the result variable. Saturation finds the stump stale, K-hit re-asks the lookup, which now hits, and the result is unified with $𝓫_c$.
] <ex-let-selection>


== Entry Point

#let entry = figure(
  caption: "The entry point.",
  stack(
    spacing: 14pt,
    flexbox(
      derive(
        "Entry",
        (
          $∅; (id, ∅, ∅) ⊢ e ⇒ τ; S₁$,
          $S₁ ⊢ Δ₁ #st_merges S₂$,
          $S₂ "fillable"$,
        ),
        $⊢ e ⇒ σ$,
      ),
    ),
    align(
      left,
      $σ = ∀(macron(α): S₁(macron(α))). ⟦S₁⟧Δ₁ ⇒ ⟦S₁⟧τ #h(3em) macron(α) = "ftv"(⟦S₁⟧τ, ⟦S₁⟧Δ₁)$,
    ),
  ),
)
#entry <entry>

The entry point is given in @entry, runs inference from the empty state and returns a top-level scheme with all its stumps attached. Instead of record the stumps, we could also simplify them using ★ but returning a scheme keeps strictly more information and is a better choice for NixLang with many exported functions. The scheme σ is read at $S₁$. The last two premises check that σ has an instance and work on a copy $S₂$ of $S₁$: the copy only extends $S₁$, so a witness at $S₂$ is a witness of σ, and no change to the copy reaches σ. The copy first merges stumps by M-merge and then passes the _fillability test_, a syntactic check of the stumps, their blockers and their results δ. Its conditions are:

+ For every spent stump p of $Δ₂$, every stump of $Δ₂$ on the blocker of p with the label of p is spent as well, and its result agrees with the result of p up to ε and associativity once every type-variable is replaced by ★.
+ No blocker reaches itself along ≻, where $β ≻ β′$ holds when a spent stump on β has β′, the blocker of a spent stump, in its result.

The first condition lets one field answer every lookup of its label on a blocker: the witness sends every type-variable to ★, and the results then coincide. The second condition orders the blockers. A sequence of steps ≻ is a _spent chain_. In `x: x.l.m ‖ {}` the spent l-stump on the row ρ of x has the result ${ρ′}$, and ρ′ is the blocker of the spent m-stump, so $ρ ≻ ρ′$. The witness fills a spent chain from its far end: it extends ρ′ by the field $m: {ρ″}$ first and then ρ by $l: {m: {ρ″} | ρ′}$. A spent chain that returns to its first blocker admits no such order, and its scheme has no instance. In `x: (x.l) x` the result ${ρ} → γ$ of the l-stump contains its own blocker ρ, so the row ρ would contain a field whose type contains ρ itself.

#let merge_rule = figure(
  caption: "Merging two stumps.",
  flexbox(
    derive(
      "M-merge",
      (
        $⟨α ⊳ ρ.l ↓ δ⟩, ⟨α ⊳ ρ′.l ↓ δ′⟩ ∈ Δ$,
        $S ⊢ δ ≐ δ′ #st_solve S′$,
      ),
      $S ⊢ Δ #st_merge S′$,
    ),
  ),
)
#merge_rule <merge-rule>

In `x: {p = (x.l) c | q = x.l}` only the first stump is spent, so the first condition of the fillability test fails. M-merge (@merge-rule) repairs this case. The rule unifies the results of two parked stumps that wait on the same blocker for the same label: the witness extends the blocker by one field, and this field answers both lookups. A merge can put two further stumps on one blocker. In `x: x.l.m ‖ x.l.m` the two m-stumps share a blocker only after the two l-stumps are merged, so the copy merges until no merge changes it. The merges run on the copy and do not reach σ. The answer to `x: {p = (x.l) c | q = x.l}` therefore keeps two results for the one lookup, and both agree at every instance, since one lookup has one answer. A-let does not merge either: no condition of A-let depends on the merge, and a use of the binding replays both stumps.

// #example(name: [A spent stump])[
//   In `x: (x.l) c`, A-sel-\? parks $⟨β ⊳ β.l ↓ δ⟩$, and A-λ-E unifies δ with $𝓫_c → γ$ for a fresh γ. The stump is spent: D-\? and D-⊥ would demand ★ for a result that is already $𝓫_c → γ$, so an instance must make the lookup hit. The program answers $∀(β: "Row", γ: "Type"). ⟨β.l ↓ 𝓫_c → γ⟩ ⇒ {β} → γ$. The instance that extends β by the field $l: 𝓫_c → γ$ discharges the stump by D-hit, so the scheme is inhabited, and this instance is the typing ${l: 𝓫_c → γ | β′} → γ$.
// ] <ex-spent>

== Inference Metatheory <inference-metatheory>

#theorem(name: [Termination of inference], lean: "runF_terminates, satStep_wf")[
  Saturation admits no infinite run, and for every term e the entry point $⊢ e ⇒ σ$ returns a verdict.
] <thm-infer-term>

#theorem(name: [Soundness of inference], lean: "runSound, run_typed")[
  If $⊢ e ⇒ σ$, then $∅ ⊢ e : τ$ for every instance τ of σ, and σ has an instance.
] <thm-infer-sound>
